Set out a rational straight line {DE}. Apply to {DE} the rectangle {DF|DFh} equal to the sum of the squares on {AB} and {BC}, and apply to {DE} the rectangle {GH|GHh} equal to twice the rectangle {AB} by {BC}. Then the whole {DH|DHh} equals the square on {AC}. [!just II.4]

Now, since the sum of the squares on {AB} and {BC} is medial, and equals {DF|DFh}, therefore {DF|DFh} is also medial. And it is applied to the rational straight line {DE}, therefore {DG} is rational and incommensurable in length with {DE}. For the same reason {GK} is also rational and incommensurable in length with {GF}, that is, {DE}. [!just X.22]

Since the sum of the squares on {AB} and {BC} is incommensurable with twice the rectangle {AB} by {BC}, therefore {DF|DFh} is incommensurable with {GH|GHh}, so that {DG} is also incommensurable with {GK}. [!just VI.1; X.11]

And they are rational, therefore {DG} and {GK} are rational straight lines commensurable in square only. Therefore {DK} is irrational and what is called binomial. [!just X.36]

But {DE} is rational, therefore {DH|DHh} is irrational, and the side of the square which equals it is irrational. [!just X.Def.I.4]

But {AC} is the side of the square equal to {HD|DHh}, therefore {AC} is irrational. Let it be called the side of the sum two medial areas.

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

This proposition is used in @X.59, @X.65 and @X.70.

The lemma is used in @X.44.
#### prop_section ####
kind: lemma
----
statement: And that <i>the aforesaid irrational straight lines are divided only in one way into the straight lines of which they are the sum and which produce the types in question</i> we will now prove after premising the following lemma.
----
proof:

Set out the straight line {AB:canvas_1}, cut the whole into unequal parts at each of the points {C:canvas_1} and {D:canvas_1}, and let {AC:canvas_1} be supposed greater than {DB:canvas_1}.

I say that the sum of the squares on {AC:canvas_1} and {CB:canvas_1} is greater than the sum of the squares on {AD:canvas_1} and {DB:canvas_1}.

Bisect {AB:canvas_1} at {E:canvas_1}.

Since {AC:canvas_1} is greater than {DB:canvas_1}, subtract {DC:canvas_1} from each, therefore the remainder {AD:canvas_1} is greater than the remainder {CB:canvas_1}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="270" height="60" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.41 lemma diagram" src="propX41l.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's lemma figure: AB cut at C and D, bisected at E. Added, all
    // invisible at rest: transparent highlight targets AC, CB, AD, DB, DC,
    // AE, EB, DE, EC (;0;0;0). The prose above this canvas binds to it
    // explicitly ({AB:canvas_1}).
    //
    // Draggables (checked against the bundle): A, B (the given line) and
    // C, D (line sliders), which only arrive with "cut the whole into
    // unequal parts", so they are deferred. E is a derived midpoint, listed
    // from "Bisect".
    var L_AB = ["A","B","AB"];
    var L_CD = L_AB.concat(["C","D","AC","CB","AD","DB","DC"]);
